# KEYMAP.md §Palette mode — open, type, run the highlighted row, `Esc` closes.
#
# One palette over everything: `:` and `ctrl-k` (⌘K on macOS) open it, a typed query ranks
# commands, worktrees, cards and agents in one list, and a leading `>` narrows it to commands.
# The rows are painted targets rather than snapshot rows, so what the query narrowed to is
# recorded in the dump (`palette.row[N]`) and the shots rather than asserted; the assertion is
# where `Enter` went.
fixture: busy
await idle 30000
assert screen == Hub && hub_tab == Worktrees

# `:` opens the palette. It shadows the Hub: one key context, one focus owner. The empty query
# lists recent sessions and suggested commands under the prefix legend.
key :
await overlay == Palette
assert mode == Palette && key_contexts[0] == Palette && focused == palette.input
assert targets["palette.input"] exists && targets["palette.row[0]"] exists
shot palette
dump palette

# `Esc` closes it and gives the Hub its keys back, with nothing run.
key escape
await overlay absent
assert screen == Hub && hub_tab == Worktrees && mode == Normal && focused == worktrees.row[0]

# `ctrl-k` is the palette's other key. A typed query is one ranked list: the best match of any
# kind is row 0, with its matched letters bumped and its own key chip on the right.
key ctrl-k
await overlay == Palette
assert focused == palette.input
type del
await idle
shot palette-ranked
dump palette-ranked

# `>` narrows to commands and the scope chip says so; `Enter` runs the top row. `Pull requests`
# is the same screen `p` reaches, which is what makes the palette checkable at all.
key ctrl-u
type >Pull
await idle
shot palette-commands
dump palette-narrowed
key enter
await hub_tab == Prs 15000
assert overlay absent && mode == Normal && focused == prs.row[0]
dump ran
quit
